@* The shared parchment popover (design-direction.md: "popovers and hover
   cards are parchment artifacts on both pages... One popover style
   everywhere -- it renders the same ExplorerPopover -- the design mirrors
   the architecture"). Renders ANY IExplorable: title, a body built from the
   Batch R section-provider REGISTRY (Explore/PopoverSections.cs), the
   pre-existing exploration chips, and a breadcrumb Stack for drilling in
   (Push) and walking back out.

   Batch R requirement 3 ("the popover becomes a content-first section
   platform"): the body is no longer one node-specific IExplorable.BodyAsync
   fragment. LoadCurrent asks PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers which ones
   apply to the CURRENT node's own Kind (Verse/Passage/Place, today); each
   applicable one resolves independently (conditional presence -- null means
   no section), and every non-null result renders, IN REGISTRY ORDER, inside
   .popover-body. A node kind NO provider claims (Chapter/Book/Author/
   TimeAndPlace/Year) falls back to that node's own BodyAsync, UNCHANGED --
   this file's pre-Batch-R rendering path for those five kinds, byte-for-byte.
   This component implements IPopoverSectionContext itself (PushAsync below)
   -- a provider's own section can drill into a new node the exact same way
   a chip already could, without ExplorerPopover growing a bespoke callback
   per provider ("no popover surgery" for Batch F/P, whichever content they
   add).

   O1 (owner live-preview correction, 2026-08-23: "explore geotemporally
   ... it's not serving us right now") RETIRED a node-specific behavior that
   used to be special-cased here (unrelated to the registry): VerseNode/
   PassageNode's own "Explore geo-temporally" chip used to reveal a mini-map
   area IN PLACE (no stack push) via any ShowMiniMap-targeted chip --
   CONTRACT's mini-map testid used to live inside the still-open popover
   beside its own chips/breadcrumb, not as a separate node, rendering a real
   <MiniWorld> (Task 15). Dead-code law took the whole mechanism with the
   chip: ExplorationTarget.ShowMiniMap (the chip's only producers were
   VerseNode/PassageNode), this file's own ShowMiniMap case/_miniMapSref
   field/<MiniWorld> render block, MiniWorld.razor itself (its only
   consumer), and its dedicated .popover-mini-map/.mini-world-map/
   .mini-world-open-world CSS -- all gone, not kept behind a flag. (Genuinely
   distinct: EventNode's OWN "Show on the map" chip also carries the shared
   popover-chip-map testid but targets NavigateWorld, not ShowMiniMap -- it
   leaves the popover for /world entirely and is UNCHANGED by O1; see
   ExplorationTarget.cs's own doc comment on the shared testid.) (The old
   popover-chip-xrefs toggle -- VerseNode/PassageNode's own retired chip --
   and its ToggleXrefs/_xrefs machinery are GONE: cross-references render
   inline now, via the registry's own CrossRefsSection, "no extra button
   press" per requirement 3(b). Same for PlaceNode's own OnSelectEvent wiring
   -- PlaceEventsSection pushes TimeAndPlaceNode via PushAsync directly now,
   so PlaceNode's own BodyAsync/OnSelectEvent survive only as its defensive,
   never-called-in-practice IExplorable fallback -- see that class's own
   comment.)

   Backdrop is a sibling element, not a wrapper, so clicking the popover
   itself never needs stopPropagation to avoid closing through a parent
   listener -- a click on the popover simply never reaches the backdrop's
   own @onclick at all.

   Batch F2 requirement 6d ("the hover windows ought not be smack dab in
   the center of the screen, but on the side of the screen where the hover
   exploration originated"): PaneAnchor (set by Reader.razor/World.razor
   from their own split state -- "reader"/"atlas"/null) anchors the PANEL
   to the ORIGINATING PANE's currently-visible region instead of the full
   viewport, while a split is open. Measured ONCE, on this instance's first
   render (mirrors PlaceCard/CardPlacement's own proven "snapshot at open,
   never re-measure on scroll/resize" discipline -- see that file's own
   comment for why a continuous tracker isn't needed here either) via
   reader.js's getPaneRect (viewport-clamped, so it's correct even when the
   reader pane's own content is taller than one screen -- see that
   function's own comment). Full-page (non-split) behavior is byte-for-byte
   unchanged: PaneAnchor is null there, so .popover-pane-anchored is never
   applied and every rule falls back to its pre-existing, viewport-centered
   base.

   O6 (owner live-preview correction, 2026-08-23, verbatim: "when you focus
   on something the background behind the hover menu gets greyed, which is
   okay, but it should be over the whole reader, rather than some weird box
   that follows your scroll and inconsistently moves around"): the
   BACKDROP used to pane-anchor too (see this file's own now-superseded
   comment, immediately above its own markup below) -- exactly the ONE-SHOT
   "measured at open, never re-measured on scroll" snapshot this paragraph
   already documents, applied to a box meant to dim EVERYTHING behind an
   open popover. That measurement going stale the instant the user
   scrolled the reader pane's own content afterward (nothing prevents
   scrolling while a popover is open) is the real, live-caught bug behind
   the owner's own report: the backdrop stayed frozen at wherever the pane
   happened to be on screen at open time -- a fixed rectangle the page then
   scrolled past/around, landing in a different, seemingly arbitrary spot
   on every fresh open depending on scroll position at that moment ("some
   weird box... inconsistently moves around"). FIX: the backdrop no longer
   reads PaneRect/PaneRectStyle at all -- always the plain, unconditional
   full-viewport `.popover-backdrop` rule (`position:fixed; inset:0`),
   split view or not, so there is no measurement left to go stale. Disclosed
   trade-off: split view's own "explore on both sides independently, the
   OTHER pane stays undimmed and clickable" (the SAME requirement 6d)
   is retired along with it -- both panes now dim together, matching every
   other popover in this app, one rule, no split-specific exception. The
   PANEL itself (pane-anchored POSITION, immediately below) is completely
   unaffected -- a popover opened from a pane still appears beside that
   pane, only the backdrop's own coverage changed. *@
@using System.Globalization
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Contracts
@using BibleAtlas.Client.State
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Views
@inject AtlasClient Atlas
@inject IExplorableClient GraphClient
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject SavedExplorationsService SavedExplorations
@inject StateAtom<FocusStack> FocusStackAtom
@inject OwnershipRegistry FocusOwnership
@inject StateAtom<IReadOnlyList<ExplorationDescriptor>> SelectionAtom
@implements IPopoverSectionContext
@implements IDisposable

@* M-D3 (R3): ShowBackdrop=false (Reader.razor passes this while the
   current popover is hover-only -- see that parameter's own doc comment)
   OMITS the backdrop ENTIRELY, not just makes it invisible/inert. This is
   the real fix for a genuine, live-caught bug: the backdrop is a
   full-viewport `position:fixed` element that appears SYNCHRONOUSLY as
   part of opening the popover, so by the time a real "click" gesture's own
   mousedown/mouseup/click/focus events fire (all AFTER the mouseenter that
   opened it), the backdrop is already covering the marker and swallows
   them -- `.reader-page`'s own `contain:layout` traps the marker's
   stacking context below the backdrop's, so no z-index fix on the marker
   alone can win that fight. Without a backdrop at all, a hover-only
   popover's own marker stays fully interactive for exactly as long as it
   remains hover-only -- a genuine click/focus reaches it cleanly, flips
   _hoverOnlyOpen false (Reader.razor), and the NEXT render correctly grows
   a backdrop for the now-persistent popover, same as clicking any other
   explorable element in this app. Also a happy side effect, not just a
   workaround: a mere hover no longer dims the whole screen at all -- "no
   auto-modal," even more literally than dismiss-timing alone would give. *@
@* O6 (owner live-preview correction, 2026-08-23): plain, unconditional
   full-viewport backdrop now -- no PaneRect/PaneRectStyle, no
   .popover-pane-anchored class, ever. See this file's own header comment
   for the full story (the retired pane-scoped variant's own ONE-SHOT
   measurement going stale on scroll is the real bug the owner reported);
   the base `.popover-backdrop` rule in app.css (`position:fixed; inset:0`)
   is now the ONLY rule that ever applies to this element. *@
@if (ShowBackdrop)
{
    <div class="popover-backdrop" data-testid="popover-backdrop" @onclick="RequestClose"></div>
}

@* M-D3 (R3), O6 UPDATE (2026-08-23): pane-anchoring no longer reaches the
   BACKDROP at all (see that element's own comment immediately above) --
   verse- and pane-anchoring now only ever compose on the PANEL, which is
   the ONLY reason they were ever said to be "mutually exclusive" in the
   first place; that finding, and the fix below, are both unaffected by O6
   and still fully apply here. A real, live-caught bug in an earlier draft:
   `.popover-pane-anchored`'s own `width`/`max-height` rule and
   `.popover-verse-anchored`'s own `top`/`left`/`max-height` rule, both at
   equal specificity, would otherwise BOTH apply to the same element (CSS
   applies every declaration from every matching rule; only same-PROPERTY
   collisions are decided by source order) -- `left`/`top` correctly came
   from verse-anchoring (declared later in app.css), but `width` has no
   verse-anchoring declaration to override it, so the panel silently
   rendered at the PANE's own width while positioned by VerseAnchorStyle's
   math (which assumes the base rule's ordinary width) -- confirmed live: a
   verse near a narrow split pane's own edge rendered with its LEFT edge
   off-screen. Verse-anchoring wins outright when present (VerseAnchorStyle
   is checked first below). *@
<div class="popover @(_verseAnchorRect is not null ? "popover-verse-anchored" : _paneRect is not null ? "popover-pane-anchored" : null)"
     style="@(VerseAnchorStyle ?? PaneRectStyle)" data-testid="popover" tabindex="-1" @ref="_panelRef" @onkeydown="OnKeyDown"
     @onpointerenter="OnPanelPointerEnter" @onpointerleave="OnPanelPointerLeave">
    <div class="popover-head">
        @if (FocusValue.Stack.Count > 1)
        {
            <button type="button" class="popover-breadcrumb-back" data-testid="popover-breadcrumb-back"
                    aria-label="Back" @onclick="Back">&lsaquo;</button>
        }
        <p class="popover-title" data-testid="popover-title" data-ref="@(IsRefKind(Current.Kind) ? "true" : "false")">@Current.Title</p>
        @* M-D3 (U3', owner: "the buttons... should be right next to the
           header... make sure it's ergonomic and fits well"): MOVED here
           from a separate .popover-chips row beneath the whole body (see
           this file's own git history for the prior markup) -- same
           _explorations/Activate wiring, unchanged CONTRACT testids
           (ex.ChipTestId), just relocated and rendered icon-scale
           (ChipGlyph below) instead of a full-text pill. The accessible
           name is still ex.Label verbatim, via aria-label + a native
           title tooltip -- an icon-only control needs both, not just the
           glyph. *@
        @if (_explorations.Count > 0)
        {
            <div class="popover-head-actions" data-testid="popover-head-actions">
                @foreach (var ex in _explorations)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="popover-head-action" data-testid="@ex.ChipTestId"
                            aria-label="@ex.Label" title="@ex.Label" @onclick="() => Activate(ex)">@ChipGlyph(ex.ChipTestId)</button>
                }
            </div>
        }
        @* Batch G2 decision 2: the save affordance -- chrome, not content
           (POPOVER-LAW-1), beside close/back per the brief. Hidden outright
           (not merely disabled) when localStorage genuinely isn't available
           (SavedExplorationsService.Available, LocalStore.Probe's own
           one-time round-trip check) -- "feature hides if storage
           unavailable," per the brief, verbatim: an affordance whose whole
           point is durable persistence has no honest disabled-but-visible
           state to offer instead. *@
        @if (SavedExplorations.Available)
        {
            <button type="button" class="popover-save-exploration@(_justSaved ? " popover-save-exploration-saved" : "")"
                    data-testid="popover-save-exploration" aria-label="Save this exploration" title="Save this exploration"
                    @onclick="SaveExploration">&#9733;</button>
        }
        <button type="button" class="popover-close" data-testid="popover-close" aria-label="Close" @onclick="RequestClose">&times;</button>
    </div>

    @* Batch R requirement 3: keyed by the CURRENT node's own Title (changes
       on every push/pop -- every node kind's Title is unique to what it
       names, e.g. a vref, a place name, a composed label) so every section's
       own rendered subtree -- INCLUDING any child component a section opens
       (VerseTextSection's own expanded/fetched-chapter state, in
       particular) -- is torn down and rebuilt fresh on every navigation,
       never silently reused across two different nodes that happen to
       render a same-shaped section in the same registry slot. A "body:"
       prefix (NOT the bare Title) -- fix, a real bug caught live, now
       HISTORICAL (O1, 2026-08-23, retired the mini-map entirely -- see this
       file's own header comment): a VerseNode's own Title and its
       since-removed ShowMiniMap chip's own SRef were routinely the EXACT
       SAME string (e.g. both "GEN.1.1"), and the since-removed <MiniWorld>
       element used to render as THIS element's own SIBLING, not a
       descendant -- Blazor's own key-uniqueness check is scoped to the
       sibling group being diffed regardless of element/component type, so
       two unrelated siblings sharing the bare "GEN.1.1" key crashed the
       renderer outright ("More than one sibling... has the same key
       value") the instant a mini-map was opened. Left prefixed rather than
       reverted to the bare Title -- correct either way now that the sibling
       is gone, and reverting would be pure churn for no behavior change. *@
    <div class="popover-body" data-testid="popover-body" @key="@($"body:{Current.Title}")">
        @if (_usingSectionRegistry)
        {
            @foreach (var section in _sections)
            {
                <div class="popover-section" data-testid="popover-section-@section.Testid">
                    @section.Body
                </div>
            }
        }
        else
        {
            @_body
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IExplorable Root { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    // Batch G2 decision 5 ("continue" -- the hamburger panel's own
    // trail-node click): when present, SEEDS the whole back-stack in order
    // (index 0 pushed first, ..., the LAST entry pushed last and therefore
    // Current) instead of the ordinary single-Root push -- "reopens the
    // popover at that node... with the trail's PRECEDING nodes as the
    // back-stack (so back works through the saved journey)," per the brief,
    // verbatim. Null (every pre-existing caller) is the ordinary single-Root
    // path, byte-for-byte unchanged. Callers that DO pass this still also
    // pass Root (EditorRequired stays satisfied) -- by convention, the LAST
    // element of this list, matching what ends up on top of the stack; this
    // component itself never reads Root when SeedStack is non-null (see
    // OnInitializedAsync below), so it's fine that they'd otherwise be
    // redundant.
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<IExplorable>? SeedStack { get; set; }

    // Batch H (split-view study, "no nested-popup rule"): true when this
    // popover instance is currently sharing the screen with the OTHER pane
    // (Reader.razor passes its own _splitOpen; World.razor passes its own
    // SplitMode) -- the one signal Activate's NavigateWorld case below needs
    // to know "a second full atlas must never open here; target the pane
    // that's already showing instead." OnNavigateWorldInSplit only needs a
    // delegate when InSplit is true (Reader.razor supplies one; standalone
    // Reader/World never do, and InSplit stays false there, so the fallback
    // Nav.NavigateTo branch is exactly today's unchanged behavior).
    [Parameter] public bool InSplit { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> OnNavigateWorldInSplit { get; set; }

    // Batch F2 requirement 6d: "reader" | "atlas" | null (the host page's
    // own split state -- see this file's own header). Which CSS selector
    // PaneRectMeasured below measures is derived from this, never hardcoded
    // -- see PaneSelector.
    [Parameter] public string? PaneAnchor { get; set; }

    // M-D3 (R3, the superscript rework): a CSS selector naming the specific
    // verse-line element this popover should anchor OVER (Reader.razor
    // passes "#v{n}" when opened via a superscript entry point; null for
    // every other entry point -- click on the verse LINE, a chip drill-in,
    // etc. -- which keep the pre-existing viewport/pane-centered behavior
    // unchanged). Measured the SAME one-shot-on-first-render way PaneAnchor
    // already is (see OnAfterRenderAsync below) -- never re-measured on
    // scroll/resize, matching PlaceCard/CardPlacement's own proven
    // "snapshot at open" discipline this file's own header already cites.
    [Parameter] public string? VerseAnchorSelector { get; set; }

    // M-D3 (R3): true (the default) for every popover except a HOVER-only
    // superscript one -- see the backdrop markup's own doc comment above
    // for the real bug this fixes (a click/focus that should PERSIST the
    // popover instead gets swallowed by the backdrop's own synchronous
    // appearance). Read on every render (not measured once) -- Reader.razor
    // binds it straight to its own `!_hoverOnlyOpen` field, so the backdrop
    // grows in on the SAME render that a click flips that field, no
    // separate signal needed.
    [Parameter] public bool ShowBackdrop { get; set; } = true;

    // M-D3 (R3): "no auto-modal that must be X'd out" -- the owner's own
    // complaint was specific to the HOVER path (a popover that appeared
    // without a click still demanded an explicit click to dismiss). Wired
    // by Reader.razor (the only caller that knows whether THIS open was a
    // genuine hover or a click/keyboard-focus -- see that file's own
    // OnXrefMarker*/CancelHoverClose comments) to cancel/reschedule its own
    // debounced auto-close as the pointer moves between the marker and this
    // panel. Harmless no-ops when unbound (every other entry point, and a
    // superscript popover that has since been made persistent by a click).
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnPanelPointerEnter { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnPanelPointerLeave { get; set; }

    // Fix round 1 (S-2 -- CRITICAL, review): "components never hold copies --
    // they render PROJECTIONS and emit INTENTS" (client/Contracts/State.cs's
    // own header, verbatim). The ORIGINAL ST-3 draft kept a permanent local
    // `_focus` field as the ONLY render source and treated the shared atom as
    // write-only (nothing ever read `FocusStackAtom.Value`) -- correctly
    // flagged as failing that sentence outright. Fixed: `FocusValue` below IS
    // the render source, and it reads the ATOM (via `_focusProjection`)
    // whenever this instance currently owns "focus-stack"; `_frozenSnapshot`
    // exists ONLY for the disclosed R4(b) exception -- a SUPERSEDED instance
    // (the ownership claim lost to a later-opened popover, R4's own
    // multi-instance reality: Reader.razor/World.razor's embedded pane/
    // MainLayout.razor's hamburger "continue" can each mount one, none
    // cross-clearing another) must keep rendering/functioning correctly with
    // ZERO visual regression for its own session, which the shared atom can
    // no longer promise it once another session owns it. `_frozenSnapshot` is
    // kept CONTINUOUSLY in sync with the atom while this instance IS current
    // (see `FocusValue`'s own getter) -- not captured once at supersession
    // time -- so it is always exactly this instance's own last-known-correct
    // state the instant ownership is lost, with no separate transition-
    // detection code needed.
    //
    // Fix round 1 (Adjudication D, item 3): restoring the G2 decision-1
    // distinction the pre-ST-3 `_trail` field's own doc comment stated and
    // this file's ST-3 migration dropped -- `FocusValue.Trail` is "every
    // node VISITED this popover session, in order" (initial open, every
    // Push, every Back LANDING per G2's own shipped rule), distinct from
    // `FocusValue.Stack` ("where back goes"; Back pops the stack but never
    // removes a trail entry). "Where I've been" vs. "where back goes" --
    // the two halves of ONE atom value now (`FocusStack.cs`'s own header),
    // mutating together per R3, rather than two independent fields.
    private Projection<FocusStack>? _focusProjectionBacking;
    private Projection<FocusStack> _focusProjection => _focusProjectionBacking ??= new Projection<FocusStack>(FocusStackAtom);
    private FocusStack _frozenSnapshot = FocusStack.Empty;

    // Fix round 2 (N-3, trivia -- REQUIRED this round, re-review: "getters
    // must be pure -- move the side effect to an explicit path"). An
    // earlier draft's own `FocusValue` getter WROTE `_frozenSnapshot` as a
    // side effect of being READ -- correct today only because every render
    // and every mutating method happens to read it at least once while
    // current, but "snapshot warmth" depended on read/render ORDERING
    // rather than being asserted at the point the atom actually changes
    // (the ownership-handoff test harness needed an extra, undocumented
    // `_ = Value;` call purely to reproduce that ordering -- a real sign the
    // dependency was implicit). `FocusValue` is a plain, pure forward now;
    // `SyncSnapshot()` below is the ONE explicit write path, called at
    // every point this instance's own view of "current" data could have
    // just changed.
    private FocusStack FocusValue => _frozenSnapshot;

    // Fix round 2 (N-3): the explicit sync -- while this instance currently
    // owns "focus-stack", `_frozenSnapshot` is set to the atom's own live
    // value; a superseded instance is a no-op (nothing to sync FROM -- its
    // own `_frozenSnapshot` stays whatever it already was, by design).
    // Called from `ApplyLocally` (right after a dispatch this instance's
    // own claim made) and from `OnFocusStackChanged` (right after a
    // reclaim, and on every OTHER Changed while still current -- covering
    // any future writer of this atom that isn't this instance itself, not
    // just self-triggered notifications).
    private void SyncSnapshot()
    {
        if (_claim?.IsCurrent == true)
        {
            _frozenSnapshot = _focusProjection.Value;
        }
    }

    private IExplorable Current => FocusValue.Current!.Node;

    // Batch ST-3 (R4): claims "focus-stack" ownership on open (below),
    // releases on close (Dispose, below) -- R4's own literal wording,
    // "claim/release follows the effect-ownership pattern on open/close."
    // `_claim.IsCurrent` decides BOTH which render source `FocusValue` reads
    // from AND which write path `ApplyLocally` takes below -- see each of
    // their own comments. The shared atom is "the ACTIVE session" (R4 option
    // (b)): whichever popover most recently claimed it, not a merge of every
    // simultaneously-open session (there is no single correct "the" focus
    // stack when more than one is legitimately open at once).
    private OwnershipClaim? _claim;

    // Batch G2 decision 2: a brief, quiet acknowledgment (CSS-only pulse,
    // .popover-save-exploration-saved) that a save just landed -- cleared on
    // the NEXT navigation (fix round 1, S-5: inside ApplyLocally now, for any
    // non-Reset intent -- see that method's own comment) rather than a
    // timer, so it never lingers or fights a fast double-save.
    private bool _justSaved;

    // Fix round 1 (S-2): the ONE place any mutating intent is applied --
    // dispatches onto the SHARED atom when this instance currently owns
    // "focus-stack" (the atom stays the single source of truth for the
    // session that matters, satisfying the compiled authority's own render-
    // a-projection rule for the owning case); applies LOCALLY to
    // `_frozenSnapshot` otherwise (a superseded instance keeps functioning
    // off its own last-known state, never touching a shared atom another,
    // now-current session owns -- R4(b)'s own disclosed exception, not a
    // silent reintroduction of "components hold copies"). Fix round 1 (S-5):
    // also clears `_justSaved` for any REAL navigation (everything except
    // `Reset`, which is a close, not a navigation) -- `RecordTrailVisit`'s
    // own retired responsibility, restored here since this is now the one
    // choke point every navigation-shaped intent passes through.
    private void ApplyLocally(IIntent<FocusStack> intent)
    {
        if (intent is not Reset)
        {
            _justSaved = false;
        }

        if (_claim?.IsCurrent == true)
        {
            FocusStackAtom.Dispatch(intent);
            SyncSnapshot(); // fix round 2, N-3: explicit -- the dispatch above already changed the atom (or was a law-2 no-op), either way this is the authoritative point to (re)assert `_frozenSnapshot`'s own warmth, not a side effect of some LATER read
        }
        else
        {
            _frozenSnapshot = intent.Apply(_frozenSnapshot);
        }
    }

    // Fix round 1 (Adjudication E residual, S-2 -- CRITICAL, review): the
    // ownership HAND-OFF. Closing the ACTIVE session Resets the shared atom
    // to Empty (Dispose, below) -- without this, EVERY other still-mounted,
    // superseded popover would be permanently desynced from the atom for the
    // rest of its own lifetime (an `OwnershipClaim`, once lost, never
    // reacquires itself). Fired on EVERY atom change (this instance's own
    // dispatches included -- harmless, see the guard below), so this is also
    // this component's only source of "someone ELSE changed shared state,
    // re-render" notifications, matching law 5 (agreement).
    //
    // "Claim = reconcile," mirroring EffectRegistry.Claim's own reconcile-
    // on-claim shape: when THIS instance is (a) not currently the owner, (b)
    // the atom just went Empty (the one value ONLY `Reset` ever produces --
    // `SeedFromTrail`/`Visit` always leave at least one entry given the
    // non-empty inputs every real caller supplies), and (c) this instance
    // still has a real session of its own to offer (`_frozenSnapshot` isn't
    // itself Empty) -- it reclaims and re-seeds the atom from ITS OWN
    // session, verbatim, via `Reseed`. Otherwise (no other live instance is
    // listening, or this instance's own session is also empty), nothing
    // reclaims and the atom simply stays Reset -- exactly the controller's
    // own ruling: "if another live popover instance exists it CLAIMS and
    // re-seeds ... otherwise reset."
    //
    // Multi-superseded-instance race, resolved by ordinary C# event
    // ordering + re-entrant Dispatch (proven safe generically by
    // ConfluenceAgreementLawTests' own re-entrant-dispatch test, ST-1):
    // Blazor WASM is single-threaded, so `Changed`'s subscriber list is
    // invoked strictly in order; the FIRST superseded subscriber to run
    // reclaims via a Dispatch(Reseed) call that is ITSELF synchronous and
    // re-entrant (it fires Changed again, immediately, nested inside this
    // very invocation) -- every OTHER subscriber, whether visited by that
    // nested re-entrant pass or later by the ORIGINAL outer pass, reads the
    // atom's value AFTER the reclaim has already landed (no longer Empty),
    // so this guard is false for them and they correctly do nothing.
    private void OnFocusStackChanged()
    {
        if (_claim?.IsCurrent != true && FocusStackAtom.Value == FocusStack.Empty && _frozenSnapshot != FocusStack.Empty)
        {
            _claim?.Dispose();
            _claim = FocusOwnership.Claim(AtomNames.FocusStack); // fix round 1, Q-4: the ownership claim key reuses the atom's own name (client/Contracts/State.cs) -- both name the same claim scope, "this session's FocusStack" -- rather than a second, independently-spelled magic string
            FocusStackAtom.Dispatch(new Reseed(_frozenSnapshot));
        }

        SyncSnapshot(); // fix round 2, N-3: explicit -- covers both the reclaim branch just above (the atom now holds exactly this instance's own re-seeded value) and the ordinary case (some OTHER dispatch changed the atom while this instance was ALREADY current, not just this instance's own dispatches)
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // Batch G2 decision 2: snapshots the CURRENT trail (not the stack --
    // Back-visited-then-abandoned nodes stay in the saved record, per "the
    // trail is where I've been"). Does not close the popover; a later save
    // (this trail having grown further since) is a brand-new entry, never an
    // update to this one -- SavedExplorationsService.Save's own "snapshot,
    // not live link" doc comment.
    private void SaveExploration()
    {
        SavedExplorations.Save(FocusValue.Trail);
        _justSaved = true;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        // Fix round 2 (N-4, trivia -- REQUIRED this round, re-review):
        // unsubscribe FIRST, before dispatching. An earlier draft dispatched
        // `Reset` (below) BEFORE unsubscribing -- since this instance is
        // still `_claim.IsCurrent` at that exact instant, `Reset`'s own
        // Dispatch synchronously invoked THIS instance's own
        // `OnFocusStackChanged`, calling `StateHasChanged()` on a component
        // Blazor is in the middle of tearing down. Harmless in practice
        // (the reclaim guard is false for a still-current instance, so no
        // OTHER side effect ran), but dispose re-entering its own live
        // event handler is exactly the hazard class this whole layer exists
        // to kill by construction, not merely by argument -- unsubscribing
        // first removes the path entirely rather than relying on the guard.
        // Every OTHER live (superseded) subscriber is UNAFFECTED by this
        // reordering -- only THIS instance's own handler is removed before
        // its own Reset fires; the hand-off (OnFocusStackChanged, above)
        // still runs normally on whichever OTHER instance reclaims.
        FocusStackAtom.Changed -= OnFocusStackChanged;

        // Batch ST-3 (R3): "close resets both" -- dispatched from HERE
        // (Blazor's own IDisposable teardown, fired for EVERY path that
        // removes this component from the tree -- RequestClose's own
        // OnClose.InvokeAsync callback chain AND every OTHER direct
        // `_activeNode = null`/equivalent site in Reader.razor/World.razor/
        // MainLayout.razor that bypasses RequestClose entirely, e.g. a
        // chapter navigation) rather than inside RequestClose itself, so
        // EVERY close path gets the reset uniformly, not just the one this
        // component's own markup happens to route through. ApplyLocally
        // dispatches this onto the atom ONLY while still current -- see
        // OnFocusStackChanged above for what happens next when another live
        // popover is listening (the ownership hand-off).
        ApplyLocally(new Reset());
        _claim?.Dispose();
    }

    private IReadOnlyList<Exploration> _explorations = Array.Empty<Exploration>();
    private RenderFragment _body = _ => { };
    // Batch R requirement 3: true when >=1 registered provider claims the
    // CURRENT node's own Kind -- Verse/Passage/Place today (see
    // PopoverSectionRegistry's own doc comment for exactly which). Decides
    // which of _sections/_body the markup above renders; a node kind no
    // provider claims (Chapter/Book/Author/TimeAndPlace/Year) leaves this
    // false and _body is exactly this file's own pre-Batch-R rendering.
    private bool _usingSectionRegistry;
    private IReadOnlyList<PopoverSection> _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
    private ElementReference _panelRef;
    private bool _focused;

    // Batch F2 requirement 6d: the originating pane's own currently-visible
    // rect, in px, measured ONCE (PaneMeasured guards this, same one-shot
    // shape _focused already uses just below) -- null whenever PaneAnchor
    // is null (full-page, unchanged behavior) OR the measurement hasn't
    // landed yet (the popover renders at its ordinary viewport-centered
    // default for that one instant, same graceful-degrade-to-default
    // discipline PlaceCard/CardPlacement already established).
    private PaneRectDto? _paneRect;
    private bool _paneMeasured;

    // M-D3 (R3): the verse-anchor's own measured rect (reader.js's
    // getVerseAnchorRect), same one-shot-on-first-render discipline as
    // _paneRect/_paneMeasured immediately above -- null whenever
    // VerseAnchorSelector is null (every non-superscript entry point) or
    // the measurement hasn't landed yet (graceful degrade to the ordinary
    // viewport/pane-centered default for that one instant, same as
    // _paneRect's own comment already establishes).
    private VerseAnchorRectDto? _verseAnchorRect;
    private bool _verseAnchorMeasured;

    // Real, live-caught bug (fixed before this ever shipped, not guessed):
    // Reader.razor's own split-pane root always carries data-testid
    // "reader-root" (its OWN CSS class toggles between "reader-page" and
    // "reader-page split-pane-reader", but the TESTID attribute never
    // changes) -- a selector targeting `[data-testid="split-pane-reader"]`
    // matches NOTHING, ever. World.razor's own root is the opposite: BOTH
    // its class AND its testid switch to "split-pane-atlas" together while
    // SplitMode. reader-root only ever needs measuring while PaneAnchor is
    // "reader" -- Reader.razor sets that whenever CompositionSplit's own
    // IsSplitOpen is true, in EITHER role, not host-only (fix round 2, N-6
    // doc rot: corrected from "while _splitOpen is true on the host" --
    // Reader's own PaneAnchor read has never been host-gated; only InSplit,
    // a genuinely different property, is). The reader-root element only
    // ever carries the split-pane-reader CLASS in the HOST role specifically
    // (a guest-mounted Reader's own outer div never gets that class --
    // see Reader.razor's own HostPaneClassSuffix use), but that class is
    // irrelevant here: this selector targets the TESTID, which is stable
    // ("reader-root") across every role.
    private string PaneSelector => PaneAnchor switch
    {
        "reader" => "[data-testid=\"reader-root\"]",
        "atlas" => "[data-testid=\"split-pane-atlas\"]",
        _ => "",
    };

    // Centers the popover ON the pane's own rect (transform: translate(-50%,-50%)
    // already applies, same as the base rule) by treating the rect's own
    // midpoint as the anchor point, and bounds its max-width/max-height to
    // the pane's own size (minus the SAME margins the base, full-viewport
    // rule already subtracts) so it can never overflow into the other pane.
    private string? PaneRectStyle => _paneRect is { } r
        ? $"--pane-center-x:{(r.Left + r.Width / 2).ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
          $"--pane-center-y:{(r.Top + r.Height / 2).ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
          $"--pane-width:{r.Width.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
          $"--pane-height:{r.Height.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;"
        : null;

    // M-D3 (R3): "anchors OVER THE VERSE... ALWAYS VISIBLE, never cut off."
    // Horizontally: centered on the verse's own midpoint (translateX(-50%)
    // in CSS), clamped so the popover's OWN ordinary width (base .popover
    // rule: min(34rem, 100vw-3rem) -- mirrored here as a fixed px estimate
    // purely for this clamp's own arithmetic, never overriding that CSS
    // value itself) can never spill past either viewport edge. Vertically:
    // "grow away from the nearer edge" (this property's own doc comment on
    // .popover-verse-anchored in app.css has the full reasoning) -- a verse
    // in the top half sets --verse-anchor-top (grows down); bottom half
    // sets --verse-anchor-bottom (grows up) -- with --verse-anchor-max-height
    // computed to fit exactly the space available on whichever side was
    // picked, so real overflow degrades to the base rule's own
    // overflow-y:auto scroll, never an off-screen spill.
    private const double VerseAnchorGapPx = 10;
    private const double VerseAnchorMarginPx = 16;
    private const double VerseAnchorEstimatedWidthPx = 544; // 34rem @ 16px root -- the base .popover rule's own min(34rem, ...) width, mirrored for horizontal clamp math only

    private string? VerseAnchorStyle
    {
        get
        {
            if (_verseAnchorRect is not { } r)
            {
                return null;
            }

            var halfWidth = Math.Min(VerseAnchorEstimatedWidthPx, r.ViewportWidth - 2 * VerseAnchorMarginPx) / 2;
            var centerX = Math.Clamp(r.Left + r.Width / 2, VerseAnchorMarginPx + halfWidth, r.ViewportWidth - VerseAnchorMarginPx - halfWidth);

            var verseMidY = r.Top + r.Height / 2;
            var openBelow = verseMidY < r.ViewportHeight / 2;

            string verticalProp;
            double verticalPx;
            double maxHeightPx;
            if (openBelow)
            {
                verticalProp = "--verse-anchor-top";
                verticalPx = Math.Max(r.Top + r.Height + VerseAnchorGapPx, VerseAnchorMarginPx);
                maxHeightPx = Math.Max(r.ViewportHeight - verticalPx - VerseAnchorMarginPx, 0);
            }
            else
            {
                verticalProp = "--verse-anchor-bottom";
                verticalPx = Math.Max(r.ViewportHeight - r.Top + VerseAnchorGapPx, VerseAnchorMarginPx);
                maxHeightPx = Math.Max(r.ViewportHeight - verticalPx - VerseAnchorMarginPx, 0);
            }

            return $"{verticalProp}:{verticalPx.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
                   $"--verse-anchor-left:{centerX.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;" +
                   $"--verse-anchor-max-height:{maxHeightPx.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)}px;";
        }
    }


    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Batch ST-3 (R4): claim BEFORE seeding -- this instance is
        // guaranteed to be the CURRENT owner the moment Claim returns
        // (single-threaded WASM, nothing else can supersede it synchronously
        // in between), so the seed dispatch immediately below is always
        // safe (never appending onto whatever a PREVIOUS session's own
        // claim left in the shared atom). Subscribed BEFORE any dispatch
        // too, so this instance hears its own claim's reconcile-equivalent
        // seeding the same way every other live subscriber would (see
        // OnFocusStackChanged's own header).
        _claim = FocusOwnership.Claim(AtomNames.FocusStack); // fix round 1, Q-4: the ownership claim key reuses the atom's own name (client/Contracts/State.cs) -- both name the same claim scope, "this session's FocusStack" -- rather than a second, independently-spelled magic string
        FocusStackAtom.Changed += OnFocusStackChanged;

        // Fix round 1 (live, self-caught bug found while writing this
        // batch's OWN ownership-handoff tests): seeds via ONE atomic
        // dispatch, `SeedFromTrail` (which ignores `current` entirely, the
        // exact same overwrite shape a separate Reset-then-Visit pair
        // achieves) -- NEVER a separate `Reset` dispatch followed by a
        // second `Visit`/`SeedFromTrail` dispatch. An earlier draft did
        // exactly that two-dispatch sequence, and it is unsafe: `Reset`
        // alone makes the atom OBSERVABLY Empty for the (synchronous, but
        // still separately-invoked) instant between the two dispatches --
        // long enough for OnFocusStackChanged's own Adjudication-E hand-off
        // logic (fired synchronously by the Reset dispatch, on every OTHER
        // live subscriber) to see "Empty" and conclude the ACTIVE session
        // just closed, reclaiming ownership AWAY from this brand-new
        // instance before it ever reaches its own seed dispatch. Folding
        // both steps into one dispatch means the atom's OWN Changed event
        // never fires an intermediate "Empty" at all during a fresh open --
        // only the FINAL, fully-seeded value, which is never mistaken for a
        // close. See client.Tests/State/FocusStackOwnershipHandoffTests.cs's
        // own header for the full trace (caught there, before this ever
        // reached the live app).
        if (SeedStack is { Count: > 0 } seed)
        {
            // Batch ST-3 (R3, G2 decision 5 "continue"): SeedFromTrail builds
            // the WHOLE session (stack + trail) from the seed list in one
            // pure Apply call, exactly mirroring the pre-atom per-node
            // Push+RecordTrailVisit loop this replaces.
            ApplyLocally(new SeedFromTrail(seed));
        }
        else
        {
            ApplyLocally(new SeedFromTrail(new[] { Root }));
        }

        await LoadCurrent();
    }

    // Focuses the panel exactly once, on the FIRST render of this instance
    // (a fresh ExplorerPopover is created per "opening" -- see Reader.razor's
    // @key -- so this naturally re-runs for every new popover without ever
    // stealing focus back on an internal push/pop/xrefs-expand within an
    // already-open one).
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!_paneMeasured && PaneAnchor is not null)
        {
            _paneMeasured = true;
            try
            {
                var readerJs = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/reader.js");
                _paneRect = await readerJs.InvokeAsync<PaneRectDto?>("getPaneRect", PaneSelector);
                if (_paneRect is not null)
                {
                    StateHasChanged();
                }
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Positioning is a nicety, not load-bearing -- same graceful-
                // degradation stance CardPlacement's own measurement takes: a
                // failed measurement just leaves the popover at its default,
                // full-viewport-centered placement.
            }
        }

        // M-D3 (R3): the SAME one-shot-on-first-render discipline as
        // _paneMeasured immediately above, independently gated (both can
        // run on the same instance -- see this file's own header comment on
        // why verse- and pane-anchoring compose).
        if (!_verseAnchorMeasured && VerseAnchorSelector is not null)
        {
            _verseAnchorMeasured = true;
            try
            {
                var readerJs = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/reader.js");
                _verseAnchorRect = await readerJs.InvokeAsync<VerseAnchorRectDto?>("getVerseAnchorRect", VerseAnchorSelector);
                if (_verseAnchorRect is not null)
                {
                    StateHasChanged();
                }
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Same graceful-degradation stance as _paneRect's own catch
                // immediately above -- a failed measurement leaves the
                // popover at its ordinary viewport-centered default.
            }
        }

        if (_focused)
        {
            return;
        }

        _focused = true;
        try
        {
            await _panelRef.FocusAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Non-fatal -- focus is a nicety (keyboard Escape-to-close, a
            // visible focus ring on open), never load-bearing for the
            // popover's own functionality.
        }
    }

    // Batch R requirement 3: builds this node's own popover body. Two
    // disjoint paths, decided by whether ANY registered provider claims the
    // current node's Kind at all (_usingSectionRegistry):
    //   - Verse/Passage/Place: every APPLICABLE provider's own ResolveAsync
    //     runs CONCURRENTLY (Task.WhenAll, same "independent fetches never
    //     serialize" rule Batch G1 already established for
    //     ExploreAsync/BodyAsync below), and every non-null result becomes
    //     one _sections entry, IN REGISTRY ORDER (List.FindAll/Select
    //     preserve source order; Task.WhenAll resolves all of them before
    //     this method returns, so the ORDER the results are READ back in --
    //     the original applicable list's own order -- is what actually
    //     renders, regardless of which fetch happened to finish first).
    //   - every other kind (Chapter/Book/Author/TimeAndPlace/Year): the
    //     exact pre-Batch-R path, byte-for-byte -- Current.BodyAsync.
    // ExploreAsync (the chips row) runs in EITHER case, alongside whichever
    // path is taken, same concurrency reasoning.
    private async Task LoadCurrent()
    {
        // Fix, a real bug caught live (diagnostic script against the actual
        // running app, not static review): Blazor's own event-dispatch
        // machinery renders once at THIS method's first genuine await (the
        // fetches below) regardless of how far LoadCurrent has gotten --
        // Current.Title already reflects the NEW node by then (_stack.Push
        // always happens synchronously, before LoadCurrent is ever called),
        // but _sections/_explorations/_body were, before this fix, still
        // whatever the PREVIOUS node left behind, so that intermediate
        // render showed the new title beside the WRONG node's content for
        // the full duration of the fetch -- reproduced live via a
        // cross-reference chain: HAB.1.7's own 6-item xref list stayed on
        // screen under a "JER.52.25" title (a verse with only 3) until the
        // real fetch resolved. Clearing here makes that intermediate frame
        // EMPTY instead of WRONG -- the same "loading, then filled" shape
        // this method's very first caller (OnInitializedAsync) already had,
        // now applied uniformly to every push/pop, not just the initial
        // open.
        _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
        _explorations = Array.Empty<Exploration>();
        _body = _ => { };

        var applicable = PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers.Where(p => p.AppliesTo(Current)).ToList();
        _usingSectionRegistry = applicable.Count > 0;

        try
        {
            var explorationsTask = Current.ExploreAsync(Atlas);

            if (_usingSectionRegistry)
            {
                var sectionTasks = applicable.Select(p => p.ResolveAsync(Current, Atlas, this)).ToList();
                await Task.WhenAll(sectionTasks.Cast<Task>().Append(explorationsTask));
                var resolved = sectionTasks.Select(t => t.Result).Where(s => s is not null).Select(s => s!).ToList();
                // Batch M-D2 (owner's cross-reference superscript directive,
                // entry-point parameter): "xrefs section LEADING" -- when
                // this popover was opened via a verse's own superscript, the
                // xrefs section moves to the FRONT of the already-resolved
                // list (a stable reorder over the SAME registry-ordered
                // result every other open produces, never a second
                // registry/ordering rule). One small, disclosed special
                // case living directly in this file, matching its own
                // established precedent immediately below (map-focus sync)
                // for a cross-cutting concern the per-node registry itself
                // has no business modeling.
                _sections = XrefEntryPoint ? resolved.OrderByDescending(s => s.Testid == "xrefs").ToList() : resolved;
                _explorations = explorationsTask.Result;
            }
            else
            {
                var bodyTask = Current.BodyAsync(Atlas);
                await Task.WhenAll(explorationsTask, bodyTask);
                _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
                _explorations = explorationsTask.Result;
                _body = bodyTask.Result;
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            _explorations = Array.Empty<Exploration>();
            _sections = Array.Empty<PopoverSection>();
            _usingSectionRegistry = false;
            _body = builder =>
            {
                builder.OpenElement(0, "p");
                builder.AddAttribute(1, "class", "popover-meta");
                builder.AddContent(2, "Couldn't load this — check your connection and try again.");
                builder.CloseElement();
            };
        }

        await SyncNarrativeFocusAsync();
    }

    // Batch N requirement 3 ("map focus sync -- the same graph, seen
    // twice"): a small, node-specific special case in this file (O1,
    // 2026-08-23, retired the ONLY other one that used to sit alongside it
    // here -- ShowMiniMap; see this file's own header comment) -- map-focus
    // is a cross-cutting concern (it targets the MAP, not this popover's
    // own body), not a section, so
    // it does not belong in the registry the way PRIOR/FOLLOWING's own
    // CONTENT does (see Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs). Runs at the
    // end of EVERY LoadCurrent (the initial open, every push, every pop,
    // every chip Activate that re-resolves Current in place) -- HOUSE
    // PATTERN: synced continuously at the point Current actually changes,
    // never captured lazily at dispose time. `INarrativeAware` (a small,
    // focused interface -- Explore/EventNode.cs, retargeted Batch T; lived
    // in the now-deleted Explore/NarrativeEventNode.cs before) is how this
    // stays decoupled from concrete node types: EventNode is the ONLY
    // opt-in today (Batch T retired VerseNode's own implementation --
    // verse-level narrative-awareness moved entirely to EVENT membership,
    // see VerseNode's own doc comment); every other node kind (Place/
    // Chapter/Book/Author/TimeAndPlace/Year/Passage/Catechism/Verse) falls
    // into the `else` branch and clears focus, so navigating away from an
    // open EventNode to anything else never leaves stale emphasis on the
    // map. Reads the SAME memoized
    // NarrativePositionsAsync the Prior/Following section providers
    // already call for THIS SAME Current node -- when both run (the common
    // case), this is a cache hit, not a second network round trip.
    private async Task SyncNarrativeFocusAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            if (Current is INarrativeAware aware)
            {
                // Batch HOTFIX-4: reads `.Narrative` only -- map focus sync
                // stays scoped to NARRATIVE arrows (there is no "timeline
                // arrow" to dim/amplify; a global-timeline-only Current
                // correctly clears focus via the `narrativeIds`/`eventIds`
                // empty-list path below, req 3's own "shared code path, no
                // special case," unmodified from Batch N).
                var result = await aware.NarrativePositionsAsync(Atlas);
                var narrativeIds = result.Narrative.Select(p => p.NarrativeId).Distinct().ToList();
                var eventIds = result.Narrative.Select(p => p.EventId).Distinct().ToList();
                await MapInterop.SetNarrativeFocus(JS, narrativeIds, eventIds);
            }
            else
            {
                await MapInterop.SetNarrativeFocus(JS, Array.Empty<string>(), Array.Empty<string>());
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Map focus is a nicety layered on top of an already-rendered
            // popover, never load-bearing for the popover's own
            // functionality -- same graceful-degradation stance
            // PaneRectMeasured/BlinkPlace already take for their own JS
            // interop calls.
        }
    }

    // IPopoverSectionContext: a provider's own section (VerseTextSection's
    // xref/date/event rows, etc.) drills into a new node THIS SAME way an
    // ExplorationTarget.Push chip already does. Explicit StateHasChanged()
    // -- load-bearing, not defensive, per the EXACT same reasoning
    // PlaceNode.OnSelectEvent's own (now-retired-as-primary) closure already
    // documented: a provider's own onclick is bound via
    // EventCallback.Factory.Create(ctx, ...) where ctx is passed as the
    // IPopoverSectionContext INTERFACE reference -- the underlying object IS
    // this component (so automatic re-render should, in principle, already
    // fire), but this call removes any doubt rather than leaning on that
    // subtlety a second time.
    public async Task PushAsync(IExplorable node)
    {
        ApplyLocally(new Visit(node));
        await LoadCurrent();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // G2-m2: the popover-internal half of the Ctrl/Cmd-click toggle
    // gesture -- same intent, same atom, same ExplorationDescriptor.Capture
    // call VerseLine's own OnLineToggleSelect (Reader.razor) and map.js's
    // marker/label toggle (World.razor) already dispatch through. Never
    // touches the popover's own stack/Current -- no push, no pop, no
    // StateHasChanged (SelectionAtom's own Changed subscribers, e.g.
    // SelectionTray.razor, re-render themselves; this popover has nothing
    // of its own that depends on selection state).
    public Task ToggleSelectAsync(IExplorable node)
    {
        SelectionAtom.Dispatch(new ToggleSelection(ExplorationDescriptor.Capture(node)));
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // Batch F2 requirement 6: see IPopoverSectionContext.OtherContextSectionCount's
    // own doc comment for why this reads `_sections` live (render time),
    // never a value snapshotted during the concurrent ResolveAsync phase.
    // "verse-text" (the subject) and "xrefs" (asking about its OWN
    // siblings, not itself) are excluded; every other resolved section
    // counts, generically -- no hardcoded catechism check.
    public int OtherContextSectionCount => _sections.Count(s => s.Testid is not ("verse-text" or "xrefs"));

    // Batch M-D2: read live off Current (render time, same as
    // OtherContextSectionCount immediately above) -- never captured into a
    // field at push time, so it always reflects whichever node is CURRENTLY
    // on top of the stack (a drill-in from an entry-point-opened popover
    // pushes an ordinary VerseNode with XrefEntryPoint defaulted false, per
    // that class's own doc comment, so the emphasis correctly does not
    // propagate past the one hop that opened it).
    public bool XrefEntryPoint => Current is VerseNode { XrefEntryPoint: true };

    // Batch P: IPopoverSectionContext.Graph's own concrete backing --
    // the injected singleton (Program.cs, M-D2), never a new instance.
    public IExplorableClient Graph => GraphClient;

    private async Task Activate(Exploration ex)
    {
        switch (ex.Target)
        {
            case ExplorationTarget.Push push:
                ApplyLocally(new Visit(push.Next));
                await LoadCurrent();
                break;

            case ExplorationTarget.NavigateWorld world:
                await RequestClose();
                // Batch H: "the split IS the atlas" -- while a split is
                // showing, a chip that would otherwise open a SECOND full
                // /world instead hands its exact query (a scripture ref or a
                // from/to window, unescaped from ExplorationTarget's own
                // ready-to-append shape) to whichever pane is already
                // showing the atlas, in place. InSplit false (every
                // standalone page) keeps today's behavior byte-for-byte.
                if (InSplit && OnNavigateWorldInSplit.HasDelegate)
                {
                    await OnNavigateWorldInSplit.InvokeAsync(world.Query);
                }
                else
                {
                    Nav.NavigateTo($"/world?{world.Query}");
                }
                break;

            case ExplorationTarget.NavigateReader reader:
                await RequestClose();
                // Batch F2 requirement 6c: preserves an open split across
                // this navigation too (SPLIT-1's own "reader navigation
                // keeps the split open" promise, extended to this chip --
                // InSplit is true for EITHER pane's own popover while a
                // split is showing, so this is correct for a chip clicked
                // from the atlas pane's own popover as well). Query BEFORE
                // fragment, same reasoning as Reader.razor's own ChapterHref/
                // ApplyScriptureRef. Batch CORPREAD-1a (SPLIT-PERSIST-1):
                // built from the ONE declared split-URL vocabulary
                // (SplitUrlContract), not a hand-rolled "?split=1" literal --
                // InSplit is ONLY ever true when Reader.razor supplies it
                // (this component's own doc comment on that parameter), and
                // Reader's only reachable host pairing is World, the SAME
                // disclosed narrowing Reader.razor's own SplitQuerySuffix
                // uses. Disclosed scope: unlike SplitQuerySuffix, this site
                // does not carry a live Follow flag forward (ExplorerPopover
                // has no ViewArrangement injection of its own) -- a narrow,
                // pre-existing gap, not newly introduced by this batch.
                var query = InSplit ? $"?{SplitUrlContract.SplitParam}={ViewNames.World}" : "";
                var fragment = reader.Verse is int v ? $"#v{v}" : "";
                var url = $"/read/{reader.Book}/{reader.Chapter}{query}{fragment}";
                Nav.NavigateTo(url);
                break;
        }
    }

    private async Task Back()
    {
        if (FocusValue.Stack.Count <= 1)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Fix round 1 (Adjudication D, S-1): the Back intent itself now
        // restores G2's own "a Back landing is a visit" trail-append rule --
        // see FocusStack.cs's own Back record doc comment for the full
        // history/fix.
        ApplyLocally(new BibleAtlas.Client.State.Back()); // fully qualified -- this method is ALSO named Back()
        await LoadCurrent();
    }

    // Batch N requirement 3: "Popover closes / context ends -> arrows
    // return to normal." Cleared HERE, synchronously at the close ACTION
    // itself (the genuine mutation point), rather than in a DisposeAsync
    // this component doesn't have and, per HOUSE PATTERN, must not gain
    // for this purpose -- RequestClose is the one choke point every close
    // path (backdrop click, the close button, Escape) already funnels
    // through, same as it always was.
    private async Task RequestClose()
    {
        try
        {
            await MapInterop.SetNarrativeFocus(JS, Array.Empty<string>(), Array.Empty<string>());
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Same graceful-degradation stance SyncNarrativeFocusAsync above takes.
        }
        await OnClose.InvokeAsync();
    }

    private async Task OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
        {
            await RequestClose();
        }
    }

    // Verse/Passage/Chapter/Book titles are canonical refs (GEN.1.1,
    // GEN.1.1-5, GEN.1, GEN) -- design-direction.md's "canonical refs...
    // set them like instrument readouts" applies to popover-title too, via
    // this attribute (see .popover-title[data-ref] in app.css). Place/
    // Author/TimeAndPlace titles are proper names/composed labels, styled
    // as ordinary prose headings instead.
    private static bool IsRefKind(string kind) => kind is "Verse" or "Passage" or "Chapter" or "Book";

    // M-D3 (U3'): the icon-scale glyph for a head action, keyed by its own
    // CONTRACT testid -- exactly the THREE distinct ids every IExplorable's
    // own ExploreAsync actually emits today (grep-confirmed across every
    // Explore/*.cs node type): popover-chip-map (EventNode's own "Show on
    // the map" -- O1, 2026-08-23, retired VerseNode/PassageNode's own
    // "Explore geo-temporally", the OTHER chip that used to share this same
    // testid; see this file's own header comment), popover-chip-book
    // ("About this book"), popover-chip-context ("Read in context"). Chosen
    // from this file's own
    // established "quiet Unicode instrument glyph, never an emoji" register
    // (breadcrumb-back's ‹, close's ×, PassageList's ▾/▲) -- a small dot-
    // in-crosshairs for a map/location action, a fleuron for "about the
    // book" (a printer's ornament, at home beside this app's own serif/
    // small-caps chrome), a pilcrow for "read the surrounding text." The
    // glyph is decorative ONLY -- aria-label/title (the markup above) carry
    // the real accessible name; an unrecognized id (defensive -- nothing
    // constructs one today) falls back to a plain bullet rather than
    // rendering empty.
    private static string ChipGlyph(string chipTestId) => chipTestId switch
    {
        "popover-chip-map" => "⌖",
        "popover-chip-book" => "❧",
        "popover-chip-context" => "¶",
        _ => "•",
    };

    // Batch F2 requirement 6d: reader.js's own getPaneRect return shape --
    // deserialized via Blazor's DEFAULT (camelCase) JS-interop JSON options,
    // not Wire.Options (this never crosses the real HTTP API either, same
    // reasoning as MapInterop.cs's own CardMeasurementDto).
    private sealed record PaneRectDto(double Left, double Top, double Width, double Height);

    // M-D3 (R3): reader.js's own getVerseAnchorRect return shape -- same
    // deserialization reasoning as PaneRectDto immediately above.
    private sealed record VerseAnchorRectDto(double Left, double Top, double Width, double Height, double ViewportWidth, double ViewportHeight);
}
